WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.201
The Dependency Ladder: What Should Live Closest to Home?. The

2
00:00:05.201 --> 00:00:10.402
right question is not “cloud or local?” It is “what

3
00:00:10.402 --> 00:00:15.603
happens if this capability is unavailable, wrong, or no longer

4
00:00:15.603 --> 00:00:20.804
under the conditions we expected?” A dependency ladder gives a

5
00:00:20.804 --> 00:00:26.005
small team a practical answer. It sorts work by consequence

6
00:00:26.005 --> 00:00:31.206
rather than fashion. Level one: local-first work. Keep durable records,

7
00:00:31.206 --> 00:00:36.407
source libraries, operating procedures, and routine drafts close to home

8
00:00:36.407 --> 00:00:41.608
when continuity and provenance matter most. Level two: allowlisted external

9
00:00:41.608 --> 00:00:46.808
work. Use specialized external tools for bounded tasks when they

10
00:00:46.808 --> 00:00:52.009
provide clear value, documented terms, and a known fallback. The

11
00:00:52.009 --> 00:00:57.210
work should still leave behind the source, output, and decision

12
00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:02.411
record you need. Level three: human-owned work. Publishing, payments, access

13
00:01:02.411 --> 00:01:07.612
changes, legal or financial interpretation, and sensitive communication require a

14
00:01:07.612 --> 00:01:12.813
named human decision. Assistance is useful; delegated authority is not.

15
00:01:12.813 --> 00:01:18.014
The ladder is a planning tool, not a purity test.

16
00:01:18.014 --> 00:01:23.215
It helps you decide where a failure would hurt and

17
00:01:23.215 --> 00:01:28.416
what a responsible fallback looks like before the pressure arrives.
